    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` \u2014 0 failures), and the change is complete.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe response already carried a *per-day* `bulkSignupWarning` flag; `capacityWarning` is a distinct *per-city* signal, so I added it as its own concern rather than overloading anything:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner schema (generates `OwnerDto.setCapacityWarning`).\n2. **`ResolveCapacityWarning`** (new function) \u2014 counts the owner's city via the existing `OwnerRepository.countByCity` and sets the flag when `count >= 40 && count < EnsureCityCapacity.CAPACITY`. It reuses the `CAPACITY = 50` constant so \"approaching the limit\" stays tied to the actual limit.\n3. **`@CapacityWarning`** (new OfficeFloor `@Qualifier` annotation) \u2014 the pipeline already flows an unqualified `Boolean` (`bulkSignupWarning`); a second `Boolean` variable collides by type, so this qualifier disambiguates it, exactly as the AGENTS.md variable rules prescribe.\n4. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 a 3-arg `toOwnerDto` overload that delegates to the existing 2-arg one and just sets the new flag (no duplication).\n5. **`RespondWithOwner` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated`** \u2014 now consume `@CapacityWarning @Val Boolean` and pass it through.\n6. **`owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.GET.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `resolveCapacityWarning` step after the bulk-warning step, under the same governance, so both create and read responses carry the flag.\n\nThe hard rejection at 50 in `EnsureCityCapacity` is untouched. Each function retains a single responsibility, and the existing bulk-warning path was left intact.",
